        ("A figure of speech that juxtaposes two contradictory or opposite terms side by side in a single condensed phrase is an:", ["Oxymoron (e.g. 'deafening silence', 'cruel kindness')", "Paradox", "Antithesis", "Metonymy"], 0, "An oxymoron conjoins contradictory words in tight syntactical union for poetic effect.", "Figures of Speech"),
        ("A statement that appears self-contradictory or absurd on the surface, but upon deeper reflection reveals a profound underlying truth, is a:", ["Paradox", "Oxymoron", "Hyperbole", "Tautology"], 0, "Paradoxes provoke reflective thought through seemingly impossible truths (e.g. 'The child is father of the man').", "Literary Terms"),
        ("A direct address spoken to an absent person, an abstract concept (like Death), or an inanimate entity as if present and able to hear is an:", ["Apostrophe", "Soliloquy", "Monologue", "Epilogue"], 0, "Apostrophe dramatically addresses intangible or deceased subjects (e.g. 'O Death, where is thy sting?').", "Figures of Speech"),
        ("A figure of speech where the name of one object or attribute is substituted for another entity with which it is closely associated is:", ["Metonymy (e.g. 'The pen is mightier than the sword')", "Synecdoche", "Metaphor", "Simile"], 0, "Metonymy substitutes associated symbols for entities (e.g. 'The Crown' for the monarch).", "Figures of Speech"),
