        ("The repetition of the same initial consonant sound in a sequence of closely adjacent words is:", ["Alliteration", "Assonance", "Consonance", "Onomatopoeia"], 0, "Alliteration enhances phonetic rhythm and musicality (e.g. 'Peter Piper picked a peck of pickled peppers').", "Poetic Devices"),
        ("The repetition of identical or similar vowel sounds within neighboring words without repeating consonant sounds is:", ["Assonance", "Alliteration", "Consonance", "Rhyme"], 0, "Assonance creates internal acoustic resonance through recurring vowel patterns (e.g. 'The rain in Spain falls mainly in the plain').", "Poetic Techniques"),
        ("The creation or use of words whose spoken sounds imitate or echo the actual noise they describe (e.g. 'buzz', 'hiss', 'clang') is:", ["Onomatopoeia", "Alliteration", "Symbolism", "Metaphor"], 0, "Onomatopoeic words mirror physical sound acoustics phonetically.", "Literary Terms"),
        ("The literary device where the physical weather or natural setting mirrors the emotional turmoil or moral mood of characters is termed:", ["Pathetic Fallacy", "Dramatic Irony", "Foreshadowing", "Catharsis"], 0, "Coined by John Ruskin, pathetic fallacy projects human emotions onto nature (e.g. raging storms reflecting King Lear's madness).", "Literary Devices"),
