        ("The accidental, humorous misuse of an incorrect word that sounds similar to the intended word (e.g. 'reprehend' instead of 'apprehend') is a:", ["Malapropism", "Spoonerism", "Solecism", "Neologism"], 0, "Named after Mrs. Malaprop in Sheridan's play 'The Rivals', malapropisms create comic characterization.", "Literary Devices"),
        ("When a speaker deliberately states the exact opposite of what they truly mean, often with a biting, mocking tone, it is:", ["Verbal Irony (Sarcasm)", "Dramatic Irony", "Situational Irony", "Hyperbole"], 0, "Verbal irony conveys underlying contempt or humor through contradictory statements.", "Types of Irony"),
