        ("A short, simple fictitious narrative drawn from everyday human situations designed to illustrate a profound spiritual or moral truth is a:", ["Parable (e.g. The Prodigal Son)", "Fable", "Allegory", "Myth"], 0, "Parables (prominent in the teachings of Jesus Christ) teach spiritual virtues through realistic human scenarios.", "Literary Forms"),
        ("A sacred, traditional narrative that explains the origin of the world, natural phenomena, gods, and religious customs of a culture is a:", ["Myth", "Fable", "Folktale", "Novella"], 0, "Myths explain cosmology, creation, and divine interventions in ancient human history.", "Oral Literature"),
        ("An object, person, color, or event in a literary work that represents something far deeper and abstract beyond its literal reality is a:", ["Symbol", "Motif", "Metaphor", "Foil"], 0, "Symbols (e.g. a dove symbolizing peace, the sea symbolizing eternity) carry rich multifaceted meanings.", "Literary Symbolism"),

