        ("Written instructions printed in parentheses or italics in a play script directing actors' movements, facial expressions, and lighting are:", ["Stage Directions", "Dialogue lines", "Prompts", "Subtexts"], 0, "Stage directions guide directors and actors regarding blocking, props, and emotional delivery.", "Theatrical Scripts"),
        ("A play that seamlessly blends dark, solemn tragic elements with lighter comic humor and irony is classified as a:", ["Tragicomedy", "Farce", "Melodrama", "Morality play"], 0, "Tragicomedies maintain serious dramatic stakes while relieving tension with comedic episodes.", "Dramatic Genres"),
        ("In drama, comic relief refers to:", ["A humorous character, scene, or witty dialogue introduced into a serious tragedy to relieve intense emotional tension", "The final applause", "The stage lighting", "The intermission"], 0, "Comic relief (e.g. the Porter in 'Macbeth', the Gravediggers in 'Hamlet') offsets dark tragedy.", "Dramatic Devices"),
        ("In traditional Greek drama, a group of performers who sing, chant, dance, and provide moral commentary on the dramatic events is the:", ["Chorus", "Orchestra", "Foil", "Antagonist"], 0, "The Chorus represents societal conscience, warning characters and guiding audience reaction.", "Classical Theatre"),
        ("A dramatic performance conducted entirely through exaggerated bodily gestures, physical movement, and facial expressions without spoken words is:", ["Pantomime (Mime)", "Melodrama", "Farce", "Operetta"], 0, "Pantomime communicates emotion and narrative purely through non-verbal physical acting.", "Theatrical Arts"),
        ("A character who undergoes significant personal growth, psychological change, or moral transformation throughout a narrative is a:", ["Dynamic (Round) Character", "Static (Flat) Character", "Stock Character", "Stereotype"], 0, "Dynamic characters evolve and learn through conflict, exhibiting realistic complexity.", "Characterization"),
