        ("The literary technique that mimics the continuous, unedited, erratic flow of thoughts, sensations, and memories running through a character's mind is:", ["Stream of Consciousness", "Dramatic monologue", "Omniscient narrative", "Epistolary style"], 0, "Stream of consciousness captures unfiltered cognitive flux (e.g. James Joyce, Virginia Woolf).", "Modernist Techniques"),
        ("The literary device of hinting at or subtly indicating future plot events before they occur in the narrative is:", ["Foreshadowing", "Flashback", "Analepsis", "In medias res"], 0, "Foreshadowing builds anticipation, suspense, and thematic inevitability.", "Narrative Techniques"),
        ("An interruption in the chronological sequence of a narrative that transports the reader back in time to an earlier event is a:", ["Flashback (Analepsis)", "Foreshadowing", "Epilogue", "Exposition"], 0, "Flashbacks explain characters' past motivations, traumatic histories, or background secrets.", "Narrative Structure"),
        ("The narrative technique of plunging straight into the middle of crucial dramatic action, bypassing preliminary background, is called:", ["In medias res", "Ab ovo", "Deus ex machina", "Denouement"], 0, "In medias res (Latin for 'into the middle of things') grips attention immediately before contextualizing.", "Plot Conventions"),
        ("An unexpected, improbable, or artificial plot device introduced suddenly to resolve an otherwise untangleable narrative dilemma is a:", ["Deus ex machina ('God from the machine')", "Climax", "Catharsis", "Foreshadowing"], 0, "Originating in Greek theater where cranes lowered gods to fix plots, it denotes contrived endings.", "Plot Devices"),
        ("A central character in a modern novel or play who lacks the conventional noble virtues, courage, or morality of a traditional hero is an:", ["Antihero", "Antagonist", "Tragic hero", "Villain"], 0, "Antiheroes are flawed, disillusioned, or morally ambiguous protagonists reflecting realistic human fragility.", "Character Types"),
        ("The underlying central philosophical message, moral inquiry, or core insight into human life explored by a literary work is its:", ["Theme", "Plot", "Setting", "Tone"], 0, "Theme represents the fundamental human truth or thematic inquiry illuminated by the text.", "Literary Analysis"),
